Fixtures of the native build's tests (tests/native-build.scala).

fixtures/<name>/  the .nir files Scala Native 0.5.12's compiler plugin wrote for Scala 3.8.4 (scala-cli compile with
                  `//> using platform native` and `//> using nativeVersion 0.5.12`, the files taken from the
                  classes directory) for eleven programs: hello, of src/hello.scala, and adts, boxing, closures,
                  codec, collections, dispatch, numeric, strings and toplevel, of tests/cases/bench_<name>.scala;
                  and nullguard, of src/nullguard.scala as it stands, no directive in the file (`scala-cli --power
                  compile --native --native-version 0.5.12 -S 3.8.4`), so that its positions are the file's lines; a
                  compile of it again writes the same files but nullguard$package.nir, the static forwarders of its
                  top-level definitions, whose definitions it may write in another order
fixtures/renamed/ the .nir files of src/renamed (package renamed), written the same way: renamed.First, which reaches
                  B (AaBB/B.scala) alone, and renamed.Second, which reaches A (AaAa/A.scala) too, of directories of one
                  String.hashCode (1f0080), so that Second's build gives B's module another name; renamed-first.expected
                  and renamed-second.expected what scalac 3.8.4's JVM build of each prints
hello.expected    what src/hello.scala prints when scalac 3.8.4 compiles it for the JVM and it runs there
                  (scala-cli run); the programs of tests/cases print what their .expected files beside them
                  hold, which scalac's JVM build printed too
nullguard.expected what src/nullguard.scala prints the same way: each read of its null receiver throws a
                  NullPointerException, where a check for null came first on one path into the read (in an
                  earlier `try`, on a branch, in the `try` whose catch case reads it) and not on another, and so
                  does `synchronized` on null (in a `try` that catches it, in a `try` with a `finally`); Scala
                  Native's own build of it (its optimizer on) dies of signal 11 at the first of those reads instead
commands.txt      the commands Scala Native's own build (tools_3 0.5.12, a debug build with the immix collector,
                  its optimizer off, for x86_64-unknown-linux-musl, hello's directory and the jars of the suite's
                  class path) ran to compile hello's C code and LLVM IR, as its log lists them, one a line in the
                  lines' order, each written as teq gives it to zig: `cc` or `c++` for clang or clang++, zig's
                  name of the target (x86_64-linux-musl), `-fno-sanitize=undefined` after `-fomit-frame-pointer`
                  (zig would otherwise check undefined behaviour in a C file compiled without an optimizer), and
                  the build's work directory written `<work>` with Scala Native's `native/` taken out of it.
                  Two are not among them: that of __buildInfo.ll, a module this build leaves empty and teq
                  does not write, and the link: teq links a static musl executable, which Scala Native does not.
descriptor-features.txt  pieces of a library's scala-native.properties (the properties grammar's separators, comments,
descriptors.expected     continued lines, escapes and line ends, bytes that are not UTF-8, Scala Native's lists and
descriptor-oracle.scala  project.gcProject): descriptors.expected is what Scala Native 0.5.12's own Descriptor.load,
                  run on the JVM, reads of each piece alone and of every two of them (src/native/descriptor.rs's
                  test reads each the same); descriptor-oracle.scala wrote it with scala-cli on Temurin 24.0.2, under
                  Scala 2.12.20 (sbt's build) and 3.3.6 (mill's, scala-cli's), and says how to run it again
library-options.expected  the cases of src/native/library-options.txt, the forms of a library's compile options a
library-options.scala     build takes: each written by library-options.scala from that table and zig 0.16.0's option
                  table, which it checks the forms against and says how to run again (`--check` compares)
jar-names.expected     what Scala Native 0.5.12's build makes of each jar of the table (refused with its exception, of
jar-names-oracle.scala no library, or the files it unpacks), by its own findNativeLibs and unpackNativeCode on the JDK:
tool-jars.py      the jars real tools wrote (tool-jars.py: the JDK's jar and its zip writers, ZipWriter.java;
ZipWriter.java    Info-ZIP, Python's zipfile, 7-Zip, sbt, Mill, Maven and Gradle, each over the entry shapes, each
                  jar's tool, version and command in the file's header), which decide the part, and crafted ones
                  (names, central directories, end records, stubs, copies, zlib's refusals of a deflate stream, each
                  beside its neighbour), whose differences are rows; the suite builds each and holds teq to it
inflate-flips.expected what the JDK's zip file system reads of 3,000 deflated entries whose streams have one to three
                  bits flipped (a fixed seed), refused or the bytes; src/native/jar.rs's test holds the copy to
                  it. jar-names-oracle.scala writes both with scala-cli on Temurin 24.0.2 and says how
trust_roots.c     the check of src/native/static_musl.c's wraps for a system's trust roots: its main, linked
libcrypto_stub.c  with static_musl.c as a build links it and with the stand-in for libcrypto the wraps call
                  (libcrypto_stub.c: a store that counts certificates, places that never hold a bundle when
                  the wraps add a distribution's); trust_roots.c says what each of its runs does

Nothing here is written from teq's output: the expectations are scalac's, Scala Native's, the JVM's and zig's.
